COMPUTER NETWORKS 
One-line definitions 

Unit I 

Computer Network: a set of interconnected devices that share data and resources.
LAN: network covering a small area like a lab or building.
MAN: network covering a city.
WAN: network covering a large geographical area, like the Internet.
Topology: the physical or logical layout of how devices are connected.
Bus: all devices share one common backbone cable.
Star: all devices connect to a central hub/switch.
Ring: each device connects to exactly two neighbours, forming a loop.
Mesh: every device connects to every other device.
Tree: hierarchical combination of star topologies.
Hybrid: combination of two or more topologies.
Hub: Layer 1 device that broadcasts incoming data to all ports.
Switch: Layer 2 device that forwards frames using MAC addresses.
Router: Layer 3 device that forwards packets between networks using IP addresses.
Protocol: set of rules for communication between devices.
OSI Model: 7-layer reference model (Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application).
TCP/IP Suite: 4/5-layer practical protocol model used on the Internet.

Unit II

Physical Layer: transmits raw bits over the medium.
Guided media: wired media like twisted pair, coaxial, fiber.
Unguided media: wireless media like radio waves, microwaves, infrared.
Attenuation: loss of signal strength over distance.
Distortion: change in signal shape due to different frequency speeds.
Noise: unwanted signal that corrupts data.
Bandwidth: maximum data a channel can carry per second.
Throughput: actual data rate achieved.
Latency: time taken for data to travel from source to destination.
Data Link Layer: handles node-to-node delivery, framing, and error control.
Parity bit: one extra bit added to make the count of 1s even or odd.
Checksum: sum of data segments sent along to detect errors.
CRC: error detection using polynomial division.
Hamming code: error detecting and correcting code using redundant bits at power-of-2 positions.

Unit III

MAC Sublayer: controls access to the shared medium.
ALOHA: stations send whenever they want, collisions are retried later.
Slotted ALOHA: sending allowed only at slot start, doubling efficiency.
CSMA: sense the channel before transmitting.
CSMA/CD: CSMA plus collision detection, used in wired Ethernet.
Ethernet: the most widely used LAN technology (IEEE 802.3).
IP Address: logical address identifying a device on a network.
Classful addressing: IP space divided into fixed classes A to E.
Classless (CIDR): addressing with variable-length prefixes, no fixed classes.
Subnetting: dividing one network into smaller sub-networks.
Subnet mask: tells which part of the IP is network and which is host.
